Or as an experienced game designer (D&D rather than nethack, mind).
Making obvious sense isn't required.  I find it more important (and
useful, and entertaining) that someone with sufficient knowledge of the
world or system can figure it out, than that someone coming off the
street does.

> People don't _like_ stuff that has to be rationalized.  It looks
> hokey.  Modern games have done away with the majority of that; while
> Nethack will never be "modern" in the purest sense of the word,
> there's no reason it can't be a bit more so or that we can't _try_ to
> clean things up as we touch them.  :)

I don't see this as a question of rationalization.  The nature of the
world is *not* the same as our own, discovering the differences should
be part of the challenge or fun.

It's important to be consistent when enough knowledge is gained.
Finding discrepancies, IME, gives players something to look for.  If
only the obvious ever happens, they can always predict results.  If
something nonobvious can happen, that gives them something (else) to
wonder about.

>> It is not necessary that a *new* player be able to predict what will
>> happen, but an experienced one should be able to.  If you consider that
>> most of the truly scary fire options don't come up until the (typical)
>> player has a moderate amount of experience, it shouldn't be a big deal.
>
> A new player won't become an experienced player unless he can make
> _some_ predictions about what will happen.

Sure.  In this particular case, though, you're not likely to run into
discrepancy or nonobvious behavior until fairly late in the game, where
newbies are unlikely to be (without being really weirdly lucky).  By the
time the 'oddness' is discovered, they should be in a position that it's
"oh, something I didn't expect" rather than "wtf? This makes no sense!"

